 After that, radar and GPS vertical rates differ by 3.6 mm a year (root mean square). That disagreement is included in every rate's uncertainty. Counting all 6 stations, including those with a very uncertain GPS rate (marked *), the difference is 3.6 mm a year. Some GPS records ended before the radar record began in 2016, so part of the difference is the two instruments measuring different years."},"nisar":{"ok":true,"summary":"NISAR's L-band radar passed over Aberdeen 28 times between November 2025 and October 2026. Adding its 27 consecutive interferograms gives the ground motion over those 336 days. Over that time the long-term rates predict that neighborhoods should differ from each other by only about 2.7 mm. NISAR's neighborhood values scatter by about 9 mm. That scatter is the atmosphere and ionosphere delaying the radar signal, not the ground moving, so NISAR cannot yet confirm or contradict the long-term rates. It needs a longer record before the noise averages down to millimeters. What NISAR shows already is reach. In a typical 12-day pair it returned a measurement over 99% of the land in these neighborhoods (between 88% and 100%), where the Sentinel-1 record gives a reliable long-term rate over 29%. Each figure uses its own product's quality flags, so this is not a like-for-like test, but it points to NISAR filling in marsh edges and wooded ground that are blank today.","product":"NISAR L2 GUNW (provisional)","doi":"10.5067/NIL2GUNW-P1","track_frame":"172_D_065","band":"L","polarization":"HH","first_date":"20251110","last_date":"20261012","days":336,"n_pairs":27,"neighborhood_scatter_mm":9.2,"expected_range_mm":2.66,"correlation":-0.15,"land_measured_typical_pair":0.986,"land_measured_every_pair":0.632,"opera_land_measured":0.289,"n_neighborhoods_compared":7,"pairs":[{"reference":"20251110","secondary":"20251122","granule":"NISAR_L2_PR_GUNW_004_172_D_065_005_4000_SH_20251110T031848_20251110T031923_20251122T031848_20251122T031923_P05023_N_F_J_001","land_mea